# TODO - Automatic prepared-media saves and animated GIF paste

## Objective

Finish the teacher's save/paste workflow without a separate manual download.

## Acceptance

- **Save and prepare** automatically persists the actual prepared rendition in
  the media folder selected in Settings. Reloading retains it; the download
  link is optional convenience, not the persistence step. Confirm the current
  behavior before changing it, and preserve existing media and recipes.
- Pasting an animated GIF preserves its animation through import, preview and
  prepared output. Prefer original animated bytes or the admitted direct GIF
  source over a clipboard thumbnail. If the clipboard provides only a static
  preview, explain how to paste the original GIF link/file rather than silently
  claiming the animation was preserved.
- Image names are unique in each library, rejecting both new uploads and
  conflicting renames without silently replacing existing user media.
  Canonical editable originals remain recoverable, but the gallery and
  prepared preview show the saved rendition by default.

Use one real save/reload check and one animated paste/export check, plus focused
regression coverage for any actual fix. Do not expand this task into an
exhaustive browser or native-platform validation project.

## Evidence

On 2026-10-21 the library application tests confirmed that `Save and
prepare` already persists the rendered bytes under the configured media
root's `renditions/` folder, without requiring a Download click. The actual
UI problem was that gallery cards still requested the unprepared source
`/media/:id`; cards now select the revision-checked prepared resource.
The original canonical source remains safely retained for re-editing.

Clipboard regressions found the static preview winning over an available
animated GIF. The picker now prefers actual `image/gif` bytes, or a validated
direct `.gif` URL when the only bitmap is a static PNG preview. Browser
clipboard formats differ, so a PNG without original GIF bytes/link cannot be
magically converted back into animation; the UI provides EN/ES guidance.

Human-visible image names are now unique per library under case, surrounding
whitespace and Unicode NFC normalization. Imports and renames reject
collisions under the existing library lock without overwriting media.

Unit tests verify each behavior. A two-frame animated GIF has additionally
passed actual native import, prepare, filesystem persistence, and metadata
reload with both frames intact. A live browser clipboard paste/preview and
UI reload check are still required before closing this task.

On 2026-10-21, the owner requested automatic saving under the configured media
folder and reported that pasted GIFs became still images. This records the
request and observed symptom; no reproduction or completed fix is claimed.
